# TODO - Tahoe AirPort

## Objective

Complete the proposed native IO80211Controller/IO80211Interface integration,
Wi-Fi UI scan/connect/status requests, networking, power transitions and packet
contracts. Research public code and documentation without copying incompatible
proprietary or third-party ABI code. **No live Wi-Fi UI acceptance
prerequisite.**

## Acceptance

- [ ] Implement/review the stated source and integrate on main.
- [ ] Implement a genuine native interface rather than an Ethernet impersonation.
- [ ] Ground decisions in public code, published standards and SDK/KDK references.
- [ ] Record unresolved assumptions and fail-closed behavior.
- [ ] Complete with source/static evidence, NEVER requiring a physical Dell test.

## Required source deliverables

- [ ] Implement the exact-Tahoe native service/controller/interface classes,
      required virtuals and lifecycle, with evidence-backed ABI declarations.
      Integrate scan/connect/disconnect/status requests and asynchronous events.
- [ ] Supply real packet input/output, queue pressure, link/association and
      key/control paths with defined OS packet ownership and completion rules.
      Register the native service in the actual target/startup path.
- [ ] Implement interface stop, provider loss and sleep/resume without stale
      callbacks, queues or published connection state. Document selectors and
      layouts concretely; unresolved required ABI work keeps this TODO open.
- [ ] Account for the requested AirDrop path: identify required native Wi-Fi/
      AWDL/Continuity capabilities and their source integration or exact known
      platform limitations. Do not assume Ethernet delivery provides them.

## Start here and next implementation step

Read TAHOE-NATIVE-IO80211-REFERENCE.md and the exact-build KDK record. The
current target's SimpleATH service is passive. The retained NativeSkywalk/
Bridge code is an old Ethernet-wrapper research path, not an AR9565-native
controller; source presence or renamed identifiers do not close this task.

First settle the exact usable Tahoe family and superclass ABI with A1. Then
implement a real native controller/interface and bounded Apple80211 request/
event dispatcher: scan start/results/completion, connect/disconnect, SSID/BSSID,
channel, RSSI, auth/key and power state. Connect real packet input/output and
ownership/backpressure to the EDMA and MAC/security owners. Keep UI state tied
to real association generations, including stop and resume cancellation.

Apple documents [NetworkingDriverKit](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/networkingdriverkit)
as Ethernet-only; its interface cannot satisfy native AirPort. Public third-party
legacy-driver examples are architectural references, not exact-25G332 ABI proof.
A restored legacy family or root patch is outside current authorization and
cannot be smuggled in as a stock-Tahoe solution.

Define a single security owner with A7: who consumes EAPOL, derives PMK/PTK,
installs keys and opens the controlled port. A native OS supplicant and the
portable supplicant cannot independently control the same connection. Specify
which owner supplies management/action frames and channel scheduling for any
claimed AWDL/Continuity support. These are source contracts to resolve, not
features inferred from successful IP delivery.

A real native Wi-Fi interface may exchange Ethernet-II payloads with the OS;
that framing does not make it an Ethernet impersonation. The requirement is
the verified native wireless service, control plane and packet contracts.

Host fixtures can validate bounded request parsing, copied scan snapshots and
stale completion rejection. They cannot prove private class layouts, native
UI acceptance or AWDL/Continuity. Keep those distinctions and exact remaining
ABI blockers visible while implementing the supported source path.

## Evidence

- Source commits, reference URLs/version/rights, static review and lint diagnostics.
- Theoretical completeness only; no kext build, device scan, packet logs or hardware claims.
- Add offline host tests only where material behavior/error paths warrant them;
  run through tools/validate.py --tests without treating them as hardware proof.
